#  Linux Users, Groups, Process Management & Services
## Objective

The objective was to learn how Linux manages users and groups, understand running processes, and manage system services.

The practical exercises were performed using Ubuntu running through Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).

---

## 1. Linux Users

Linux is a multi-user operating system. Multiple users can have accounts on the same Linux system, with different permissions and levels of access.

I used the following command to identify the current user:

```bash
whoami
The id command displays information about a user, including:

User ID (UID)
Group ID (GID)
Group memberships
2. Creating a Linux User

I created a practice user called intern using:

sudo adduser intern

Ubuntu created the user's home directory and primary group.

I verified the user using:

id intern

The result showed:

uid=1003(intern) gid=1003(intern) groups=1003(intern),100(users)

The exact UID and GID are system-specific.

Understanding UID and GID
UID — User ID, a numerical identifier assigned to a Linux user.
GID — Group ID, a numerical identifier assigned to a Linux group.
3. Linux User Home Directories

I checked the /home directory using:

ls /home

The intern user's home directory was created at:

/home/intern

I also used:

ls -la /home/intern

to inspect the contents of the user's home directory.

4. Linux Groups

Linux groups make it easier to manage permissions for multiple users.

Instead of assigning permissions individually to many users, users can be placed into a group and permissions can be assigned to the group.

For example:

developers
├── User 1
├── User 2
└── User 3
5. Creating a Group

I created a group called developers using:

sudo groupadd developers

I verified the group using:

getent group developers
6. Adding a User to a Group

I added the intern user to the developers group using:

sudo usermod -aG developers intern

The -aG options append the specified group to the user's supplementary groups.

I verified the user's group memberships using:

groups intern

The user was shown as a member of the developers group.

7. Linux Processes

A process is a running instance of a program.

Examples of programs that can run as processes include:

Shells
Web servers
Applications
Databases
System utilities

Each process has a unique Process ID (PID).

8. Viewing Processes with ps

I used:

ps

to display processes associated with the current terminal/session.

I also used:

ps aux

to display a more detailed list of running processes.

Important information displayed by process-management commands can include:

User
Process ID (PID)
CPU usage
Memory usage
Running command
9. Monitoring Processes with top

I used:

top

to monitor running processes in real time.

top provides information such as:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Process IDs
Running processes
System activity

I exited the top interface using:

q
10. Process Termination

Linux provides the kill command to send a signal to a process.

The general syntax is:

kill PID

For example:

kill 1234

where 1234 represents the PID of the process.

Processes should only be terminated when the administrator understands what the process is and what effect stopping it may have.

11. Linux Services

A service is a program that runs in the background and provides functionality to the operating system or other applications.

Examples include:

SSH
Web servers
Database services
Other system services

Linux systems using systemd commonly use systemctl to manage services.

12. Managing Services with systemctl
Check service status
systemctl status service-name
Start a service
sudo systemctl start service-name
Stop a service
sudo systemctl stop service-name
Restart a service
sudo systemctl restart service-name
Enable a service at boot
sudo systemctl enable service-name
Disable a service at boot
sudo systemctl disable service-name
13. Listing Services

I used:

systemctl list-units --type=service

to view services known to the system.

The exact services available can differ depending on the Linux environment.
